A||||16||Alex4mula||Diff between 85 GT & 87 SE||||1||
Z||000000||Alex4mula||03-13-2001||03:09 PM||||Other than model name which difference exist between these two year models? Tks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Galen||03-13-2001||04:28 PM||||one was made in 85 the other in 87..||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||03-13-2001||04:58 PM||||Galen is good -- that translates to two years difference, right Galen?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||03-13-2001||05:04 PM||||<BR>Try this <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/articles/differences.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/articles/differences.html</A> <P>hope it helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||2xsess||03-13-2001||06:07 PM||||ok, there are some other differences, the 87SEs could be got with a 2.8 or 2.5. They also had the black ground effects whereas the 85GTs had gray ground effects. The 86-87SEs had the aux gauges. Oh and the 87SEs could also have the performance sound option.<P>According to the Gary Witzenburg Fiero book 85-86 2.8s had 140hp w/ 170ft lbs of torque, the 87 2.8s had 135hp w/ 165 ft lbs. of torque.<P>there were 22,534 85GTs made<BR>and 32,305 87SEs made.<P>The 87 had 1.7 gal more fuel capacity.<BR>Curb weights<BR>85GT 2572 lbs.<BR>87SE 2567 lbs.<P>Steering Ratio<BR>85GT 19:2:1<BR>87SE 20:0:1<P>Standard Tire Sizes<BR>85GT 215/60-R14<BR>87SE 195/70-R14<P>Oh and the 87 was available with the 5 speed manual tranny.<P>Hope I covered what you were looking for.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">       <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A><p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 03-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||03-13-2001||06:50 PM||||The 85's have ribbed body-side molding, and the 87's have smooth moldings.<P>Oh yeah, the difference in power ratings was just for emmisions/noise level reasons.  87 and 88 2.8's do have an improved oiling system and the 88's are internally balanced.  The 87-88 also had "Fiero" engraved into the top of the plenum, while the 85-86 have a sticker.<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 03-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||03-13-2001||06:51 PM||||Most differences were the 87 SE came stock with a 4-cyl and 195/70R14 tires. But the V-6 option added the 215/60R14 tires. The other things missed are:<BR>Sail panel speakers in 87<BR>seat speakers in 85<BR>87 had premium sound option (subwoofer)<BR>87 had gages above radio pod. <BR>87 gray interiors were darker gray than the 85's.<BR>Sunroof channel on 87 had drain holes 85 did not.<BR>85 muncie 4-spd<BR>87 Getreg 5-spd (with V-6)<P> <BR>Standard, the SE's still had the ribbed molding. Only the coupe changed to smooth (Fastback GT's always had smooth)<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||03-13-2001||07:04 PM||||The SE's still had ribbed?  I thought after 86 all the Aero body's had smooth molding.||||reg||1
Z||000008||RRobinson||03-13-2001||07:23 PM||||Actually, the production #s are:<P>85 GT      22,534<BR>87 SE I4    1,401<BR>87 SE V6    2,474 <P>All aero notchbacks had the ribbed moulding.<P>87 SEs also had the "SE" 4 spoke steering wheel in leather or not. GTs always had the 3 spoke'r.<P>Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000009||rodmcneill||03-13-2001||11:21 PM||||Also 87-88s have quieter more reliable headlight motors. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||03-13-2001||11:37 PM||||Wow,<P>Thats the most thorough answer I have ever seen for a question!<P>LOL||||reg||1
Z||000011||Haze_Performance||03-13-2001||11:55 PM||||Also the 85 GT had an sidelit 85mph speedo & the 87 SE has a backlit 120mph speedo (V6) Right?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">  </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>PFF Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery (Updated Weekly)</A>||||reg||5
Z||000012||Alex4mula||03-14-2001||08:15 AM||||Wow! Great detail answer! Thanks all. I was looking into an 85 GT as a 2nd Fiero but now that you mention I don't like the 85 speedo, ribbed mouildings, & speakers in the seat. I think I'll pass this and keep looking for an 88 GT. Thansk all again.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||03-14-2001||03:09 PM||||Don't forget...'86 and up had a 3rd brake light.  <P>Alex4mula--You'll pay a hell of a lot more for an '88 GT.  Body style difference, too.  If you're looking for a notchback/aero, I think the '85 GT is the way to go.  Spend a few bucks and swap parts.  My '85 GT has the 120mph backlit gauges and the aux gauges.  Didn't cost that much, either.  Just my 2/100ths of a buck.||||reg||1
Z||000014||2xsess||03-14-2001||03:21 PM||||Alex, I'm selling my 85GT, it has the 120mph speedo and Aux gauges in it.<P>$2000 obo.<P>I have roughly $2200 in it right now, including new slave cylinder and left side drive axle.<P>More pics and info on my site.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Alex4mula||03-14-2001||04:38 PM||||Thanks for the offer 2axxes. <BR>Monkey; I know I'm gonna pay more for an 88. I may consider a pre-88 fastback but I'm so familiar with 88s (my Formula) that I would pay a little more for that. Anyway if I find a very good deal on a 86-7 FB I would take it. I saw a good deal on an 86 the other day but the car was all rusted underneath. My plan is to buy one for a V8 project and for long term ownership (forever!)||||reg||1
Z||000016||RRobinson||03-14-2001||05:13 PM||||Couldn't resist this one...<P>85 GTs has the WS6 suspension.<BR>87 SEs had the Y99 "Rally" suspension.<P>I'm not sure of the actual difference in the hardware (if any).<P>Also, my 87 SE V6 is placarded and order coded for the P195/70R14 tires instead of the P215/60R14s that are supposed to be standard on V6 cars. Interesting.<P>Ryan ||||reg||12
